passages.text:
For more than a century, the keepers of a lighthouse on the north shore of Lake Superior wrote down the weather every few hours. {{1|Their logbooks, now stored in a state archive,}} record wind direction, wave height, and the color of the sky at dawn. To the keepers, these notes were routine. {{2|Similarly,}} to climate scientists today they are treasures.

Historians first noticed the logbooks' value in the 1990s, when a graduate student {{3|digitizing}} old shipping records found that the keepers' entries matched storm reports from passing freighters. {{4|Unlike a freighter,}} the logs offered something the ships could not: an unbroken record from a single place. A ship passes through a storm in a few hours, but a lighthouse {{5|stays where it is, and it does not move,}} season after season.

Turning handwritten notes into usable data {{6|has proven}} slow work. Each keeper had a personal shorthand. One wrote "NW fr" for a fresh northwest wind; another simply drew an arrow. Volunteers now transcribe the pages online, and each page {{7|is checked by at least two different people before its numbers are added to the database.}}

The effort is already paying off. Comparing the keepers' records with modern measurements, researchers {{8|have figured out}} that the lake now freezes, on average, about two weeks later than it did a century ago. {{9|The keepers, who never imagined their notes would be read this way, simply wanted to keep the light burning and the ships safe.}} {{10|Their careful habits have given scientists a rare gift: a long view of a changing lake.}}
